1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,130
Ruth: Ed, how are you getting on with the reading for our presentation next week?

2
00:00:08,130 --> 00:00:11,440
Ed: Well, okay Ruth, but there's so much of it.

3
00:00:11,440 --> 00:00:16,960
Ruth: I know. I hadn't realized birth order was such a popular area of research.

4
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:21,300
Ed: But the stuff on birth order and personality is mostly unreliable.

5
00:00:21,300 --> 00:00:23,126
From what I've been reading,

6
00:00:23,126 --> 00:00:30,064
a lot of the claims about how your position in the family determines certain personality traits are just stereotypes,

7
00:00:30,064 --> 00:00:32,620
with no robust evidence to support them.

8
00:00:32,620 --> 00:00:35,640
Ruth: Okay, but that's an interesting point.

9
00:00:35,640 --> 00:00:39,830
We could start by outlining what previous research has shown.

10
00:00:39,830 --> 00:00:42,730
There are studies going back over 100 years.

11
00:00:42,730 --> 00:00:47,220
Ed: Yeah, so we could just run through some of the typical traits,

12
00:00:47,220 --> 00:00:51,711
like the consensus seems to be that oldest children are generally less well-adjusted

13
00:00:51,711 --> 00:00:55,510
because they never get over the arrival of a younger sibling.

14
00:00:55,510 --> 00:00:57,960
Ruth: Right, but on a positive note

15
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:01,390
some studies claim that they were thought to be good at nurturing.

16
00:01:01,390 --> 00:01:04,274
Certainly in the past, when people had large families,

17
00:01:04,274 --> 00:01:07,800
they would have been expected to look after the younger ones.

18
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:10,768
Ed: There isn't such a clear picture for middle children,

19
00:01:10,768 --> 00:01:16,109
but one trait that a lot of the studies mention is that they are easier to get on

20
00:01:16,109 --> 00:01:17,890
with an older or younger siblings.

21
00:01:17,890 --> 00:01:20,780
Ruth: Mm, generally eager to please and helpful

22
00:01:21,060 --> 00:01:25,065
although that's certainly not accurate as far as my family goes,

23
00:01:25,065 --> 00:01:26,920
my middle brother was a nightmare

24
00:01:27,420 --> 00:01:30,730
always causing fights and envious of whatever I had.

25
00:01:30,730 --> 00:01:34,610
Ed: As I said, none of these seems to relate to my own experience.

26
00:01:34,610 --> 00:01:36,360
I'm the youngest in my family

27
00:01:36,360 --> 00:01:40,070
and I don't recognize myself in any of the studies I've read about.

28
00:01:40,070 --> 00:01:45,957
I'm supposed to have been a sociable and confident child who made friends easily,

29
00:01:45,957 --> 00:01:48,480
but I was actually terribly shy.

30
00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:50,590
Ruth: Really? that's funny.

31
00:01:50,590 --> 00:01:52,980
There have been hundreds of studies on twins

32
00:01:53,320 --> 00:01:55,970
but mostly about nurture versus nature.

33
00:01:55,970 --> 00:01:58,080
Ed: There was one on personality

34
00:01:58,080 --> 00:02:02,801
which said that a twin is likely to be quite shy in social situations,

35
00:02:02,801 --> 00:02:06,900
because they always have their twin around to depend on for support.

36
00:02:06,900 --> 00:02:08,661
Ruth: My cousins were like that.

37
00:02:08,661 --> 00:02:11,940
When they were small, they were only interested in each other

38
00:02:11,940 --> 00:02:14,530
and found it hard to engage with other kids.

39
00:02:14,530 --> 00:02:16,070
They're fine now though.

40
00:02:16,070 --> 00:02:19,650
Ed: Only children have had a really bad press.

41
00:02:19,650 --> 00:02:22,347
A lot of studies have branded them as loners

42
00:02:22,347 --> 00:02:24,540
who think the world revolves around them

43
00:02:24,540 --> 00:02:27,440
because they've never had to fight for their parents' attention.

44
00:02:27,440 --> 00:02:29,760
Ruth: That does seem a bit harsh.

45
00:02:29,760 --> 00:02:34,840
One category I hadn't considered before was children with much older siblings.

46
00:02:34,840 --> 00:02:39,000
A couple of studies mention that these children grow up more quickly

47
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:43,160
and are expected to do basic things for themselves like getting dressed.

48
00:02:43,160 --> 00:02:45,380
Ed: I can see how that might be true

49
00:02:45,780 --> 00:02:49,679
although I expect that sometimes the exact opposite,

50
00:02:49,679 --> 00:02:53,130
playing the baby role, from clamoring for special treatment.

51
00:02:53,130 --> 00:02:56,650
Ruth: What was the problem with most of these studies, do you think?

52
00:02:56,650 --> 00:02:59,780
Ed: I think it was because in a lot of cases

53
00:03:00,340 --> 00:03:03,968
data was collected from only one sibling per family,

54
00:03:03,968 --> 00:03:09,090
who rated him or herself and his or her siblings at the same time.

55
00:03:09,090 --> 00:03:14,217
Ruth: Mm, some of the old research into the relationship between birth order

56
00:03:14,217 --> 00:03:18,160
and academic achievement has been proved to be accurate though.

57
00:03:18,160 --> 00:03:25,500
Performances in intelligence tests declined slightly from the eldest child to his or her younger siblings.

58
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:28,390
This has been proved in lots of recent studies.

59
00:03:28,390 --> 00:03:33,860
Ed: Yes, although what many of them didn't take into consideration was family size.

60
00:03:33,860 --> 00:03:35,420
The more siblings there are

61
00:03:35,440 --> 00:03:40,290
the more likely the family is to have a low socioeconomic status,

62
00:03:40,290 --> 00:03:44,450
which can also account for differences between siblings in academic performance.

63
00:03:44,450 --> 00:03:49,050
Ruth: The oldest boy might be given more opportunities than his younger sisters, for example.

64
00:03:49,050 --> 00:03:50,020
Ed: Exactly.

65
00:03:50,020 --> 00:03:57,510
Ruth: But the main reason for the marginally higher academic performance of oldest children is quite surprising, I think.

66
00:03:57,510 --> 00:04:02,503
It's not only that they benefit intellectually from extra attention at a young age,

67
00:04:02,503 --> 00:04:05,000
which is what I would have expected.

68
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:10,530
It's that they benefit from being teachers for their younger siblings by verbalizing processes.

69
00:04:10,530 --> 00:04:14,433
Ed: Right. And this gives them status and confidence,

70
00:04:14,433 --> 00:04:18,770
which again contributes in a small way to better performance.

71
00:04:18,770 --> 00:04:23,570
So would you say sibling rivalry has been a useful thing for you.

72
00:04:23,570 --> 00:04:24,950
Ruth: I think so.

73
00:04:24,950 --> 00:04:28,800
My younger brother was incredibly annoying and we fought a lot

74
00:04:29,180 --> 00:04:31,900
but I think this has made me a stronger person.

75
00:04:31,900 --> 00:04:34,140
I know how to defend myself.

76
00:04:34,140 --> 00:04:38,852
We had some terrible arguments and I would have died rather than apologize to him,

77
00:04:38,852 --> 00:04:41,680
but we had to put up with each other.

78
00:04:41,680 --> 00:04:45,210
And most of the time we coexisted amicably enough.

79
00:04:45,210 --> 00:04:47,800
Ed: Yes, my situation was pretty similar

80
00:04:48,500 --> 00:04:53,280
but I don't think having two older brothers made me any less selfish.

81
00:04:53,280 --> 00:04:56,750
I was never prepared to let my brothers use any of my stuff.

82
00:04:56,750 --> 00:05:00,000
Ed: That's perfectly normal, whereas some time... (fade out)
